% magnetostatic2d — 2-D linear magnetostatics, A-formulation: currents along z, field in the x–y plane
%
% Usage:      r = magnetostatic2d(m, mur, J)                 A = 0 on the whole outer boundary
%             r = magnetostatic2d(m, mur, J, bc, opts)
%   m         a quadGrid mesh (equal rectangles; SI: metres)
%   mur       relative permeability: one number or one per element (air 1, iron 1000 … linear only)
%   J         current density along z (A/m²): one number or one per element (+ out of the page, − into it)
%   bc        struct, optional: bc.fixed = { struct('nodes', n, 'value', A0), … } (fixed A_z, Wb/m). Without
%             bc the outer boundary is held at A = 0 (flux parallel to it: a closed box). An edge left free is
%             natural (ν ∂A/∂n = 0: the field crosses it at right angles — a symmetry plane of the current, or an
%             "open" end). On a symmetry plane where the current is mirrored with opposite sign, hold A = 0.
%   opts      struct, optional: show (true)
% Returns:    r.A (nodes × 1, Wb/m), r.B (elements × 2: Bx = ∂A/∂y, By = −∂A/∂x at the element centres, T),
%             r.Bmag, r.energy (½∫ν|B|² per unit depth, J/m), r.fields {A (node), B (element, 3 components)}.
% Method:     −∇·(ν ∇A_z) = J_z with ν = 1/(μ0 μr): the same operator as poisson2d (it is called with c = ν,
%             s = J), then B = ∇ × (A_z ẑ). Bilinear elements; B is constant-ish per element (evaluated at the centre).
% Units:      SI: m, A/m², T, Wb/m; μ0 = 4π·10⁻⁷ H/m.
% Assumes:    2-D (infinitely long in z), static, linear materials (no saturation), no eddy currents.
% Limits:     equal rectangles only; an iron part needs the mesh fine at its edges; no permanent magnets.
% References: P. P. Silvester, R. L. Ferrari, Finite Elements for Electrical Engineers (3rd ed., 1996) ch. 3
%             (vector potential in 2-D); J. D. Jackson, Classical Electrodynamics (3rd ed.) §5.1–5.4 (a long
%             solenoid: B = μ0 n I inside); two current sheets of strip length 2L at ±a give
%             B(0) = (2/π) μ0 K atan(L/a) at the centre (Biot–Savart for infinite strips).
% Check:      a long "solenoid" in 2-D: two current sheets K = n I = 1000 A/m at x = ±1 (0.25 thick), 80 long, a
%             quarter model (A = 0 on the plane x = 0 between the opposite currents, natural on y = 0, on the open
%             ends and on the outer side x = 4, which then acts as an ideal return path of zero reluctance):
%             B at the centre vs μ0 n I within 2 % (measured 0.001 %); coil and an iron core μr = 100 running the
%             full length of the model (an endless solenoid): B = μr μ0 n I (bin/test-craftscript.mjs --lib). With A = 0 on a box around a solenoid in open air the
%             finite-length value (2/π)·atan(L/a) is approached as the box grows (16 × 80: −5.5 %, 32 × 100: −2.1 %).
function r = magnetostatic2d(m, mur, J, bc, opts)
if nargin < 5
  opts = struct();
end
if nargin < 4 || ~isfield(bc, 'fixed')
  X = meshNodes(m);
  lo = min(X); hi = max(X);
  tol = 1e-9 * max(hi - lo);
  outer = find(abs(X(:, 1) - lo(1)) < tol | abs(X(:, 1) - hi(1)) < tol | abs(X(:, 2) - lo(2)) < tol | abs(X(:, 2) - hi(2)) < tol);
  bc = struct();
  bc.fixed = {struct('nodes', outer, 'value', 0)};
end
mu0 = 4e-7 * pi;
nu = 1 ./ (mu0 * mur);
p = poisson2d(m, nu, J, bc, struct('show', false, 'name', 'A'));
B = [p.grad(:, 2), -p.grad(:, 1)];
r = struct();
r.A = p.phi;
r.B = B;
r.Bmag = sqrt(sum(B .^ 2, 2));
r.energy = p.energy;
ne = size(B, 1);
r.fields = {field(m, p.phi, 'node', 'A_z', 'Wb/m'), field(m, [B, zeros(ne, 1)], 'elem', 'B', 'T')};
fprintf('magnetostatic2d: %d elements, |B| up to %.4g T, energy %.4g J/m\n', ne, max(r.Bmag), r.energy);
if getopt(opts, 'show', true)
  show(r.fields{2}, 'name', 'magnetostatic2d: B');
end
end

function v = getopt(s, name, default)
if isfield(s, name)
  v = s.(name);
else
  v = default;
end
end
